From 21775f1ca7301b3e644ceaa47df90f2b262e8d95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:06:25 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Aurora: in-between frames through fast camera turns

A fast turn (a mouse camera swinging tens of degrees in a game frame) moved
distant scenery past the plausibility bounds, so up to a third of frames
were taken for camera cuts and shown at 30, and draws that failed on their
own jumped against the blended scene around them (a doubled look).

- The camera's motion (voted for by the draws with a key of their own, now
  before plausibility, or the frame before's until enough have voted) is
  taken out of each pair: plausibility judges only the object's own motion,
  and the in-between matrices are that motion blended in the previous
  camera's view and carried by exactly half of the camera's motion
  (quaternion half-turn; D^0.5 * D^0.5 = D), so scenery lands where the
  halfway camera sees it however far it turned, without the shrink of a
  straight blend of two views. Lights and normal matrices follow.
- A draw with a key of its own that changed rigidly but past the bounds is
  blended by half of its own motion (the sky, the first rooms of a turn).
- Copies of a model are found through a grid of where the camera carries the
  previous frame's copies, instead of a budgeted scan that a turn bringing
  dozens of copies into view used up; a moved copy pairs only within an
  actor's pace.
- Copies just come into view do not count toward a cut while the camera's
  motion is a turn (a field of grass swung into view at once).
- Motions over 70 degrees or 1000 units stay cuts. DOL_AURORA_FRAME_INTERP_LOG_FRAMES
  logs a line per game frame.

Scripted mouse turns at 11-32 degrees a game frame: 355 of 355 frames
interpolated (5 before), 0.65 percent of draws unblended (5.9). Unit tests
cover a 50-degree turn, a walker during it, a field coming into view and a cut.
